body:
# Machine Epistemics lifetime-scaling theory lane

Trigger: `SzeChunYiu/ORION-OCM#50`  
Companions: #202 (core–memory–time–verification resource frontier), #353 (field completion), #304 (science ↔ machine work division).

## Role

ORION-OCM #50 has sharpened the machine-level research target into a joint lifelong hypothesis: persistent explicitly warranted executable competence may produce amortized acquisition, task-relevant computation, exact local revision and a different cumulative resource regime. Per #304, **this issue owns only the formal science** needed to explain/falsify those signatures. OCM owns runtime implementation and empirical receipts.

The critical parent-subtraction result from the first #50 calibration is already negative in a useful way:

- reusable executable skills have strong skill-library parents;
- sparse query access has ANN/indexed-retrieval parents;
- exact local revision/revocation has TMS/ATMS parents.

Therefore no theorem may call one of those mechanisms individually a Machine-Epistemics residual merely because OCM implements it. The interesting object is the governed lifelong coupling, if anything remains after strongest-parent composition.

## T1 — formal state/active-subspace objects

Define architecture-relative persistent state size `N_G(K)` under an explicit object grammar `G`, storage-size coordinates, and an operation trace whose identity-bearing persistent touch set defines `k_G(q,K)`.

Required distinctions:

- logical object count vs serialized bytes vs immutable/static model description;
- semantic object touches vs index probes vs repeated work;
- returned top-k/non-zero activation/incidence count **not** automatically equal to `k`;
- uninstrumented global passes imply CANNOT_CHECK for an active-subspace claim.

Prove invariance/monotonicity properties that are actually warranted, or record impossibilities under grammar refinement.

## T2 — when can query cost depend on `k` rather than `N`?

Give upper/lower bounds for exact finite navigation/search families with explicit index-construction and maintenance coordinates.

Attack at least:

1. global/dense state update;
2. sparse adjacency but all-row iteration;
3. frontier/local traversal;
4. ANN/indexed retrieval parent;
5. cached/materialized dependency indexes.

A current OCM observation motivating the theory: its sparse navigation stores sparse incidences but the fixed-point iteration still updates every state row. Formalize when adjacency sparsity is insufficient for `O(k)` or sub-`N` operation.

Admissible terminals include `PARENT_OWNED_INDEXED_RETRIEVAL`, `SPARSE_STRUCTURE_NOT_SPARSE_EXECUTION`, an exact trade-off frontier, or a genuine residual theorem if earned.

## T3 — local revision/revocation theorem with strongest parent

Model minimal sufficient support/alternate support and define:

- semantic change/reopening cone;
- touched verification cone;
- stale survivor and collateral invalidation errors;
- restoration/relearning correspondence.

Prove the exact finite locality result relative to ATMS/TMS/reason-maintenance parents first. Determine whether Machine Epistemics adds any formal obligation beyond parent-owned reason maintenance when dependencies extend through executable operators, cached products, scopes/authority and lifetime state.

`PARENT_OWNED` is an expected valid outcome.

## T4 — reusable competence and acquisition-cost reduction

Define a reusable executable method object and a task family with an explicit information channel. Characterize conditions under which prior knowledge `K_t` can reduce new information/work to reach a fixed competence threshold:

`C_acquire(T_j | K_t) < C_acquire(T_j | K_0)`.

The theorem must expose where the saving comes from (shared method information, search reduction, verifier reuse, representation reuse, etc.) and compare against a strongest explicit skill-library / case-based / continual-adaptation parent with the same reusable content.

Seek lower bounds and impossibility cases as well as sufficient conditions.

## T5 — strongest-parent product / joint residual

Construct the strongest faithful parent product from known components, at minimum conceptually:

`indexed retrieval × persistent structured memory × executable skill library × continual adaptation × truth/reason maintenance × exact verifier access`.

Question: under matched information/resource coordinates, which Machine Epistemics behaviors are simulated by this product? State a parent-product equivalence theorem where possible. A scientific residual exists only for obligations not reproduced by the product, not for the components individually.

This row is the theoretical counterpart of #50's P6 comparator and should connect directly to FDX-15 / parent-product equivalence in #353.

## T6 — cumulative lifetime resource frontier

Define cumulative vector cost over acquire/use/revise phases without hiding preprocessing, index maintenance, storage, verification or external IO. Characterize coordinate-specific crossover conditions and Pareto dominance. Do not prove a scalar "cheaper AI" result by selecting favorable weights after the fact.

Connect to #202's core–memory–time–verification frontier.

## Exact-study requirements

For each theorem/law candidate:

- strongest parent named before testing;
- exact finite calibration where applicable;
- positive, no-alarm, hostile mutant and CANNOT_CHECK path;
- counterexample search before theorem promotion;
- asymptotic statement separated from finite implementation receipt;
- status one of `PROVED`, `PARENT_OWNED`, `EXACTLY_BOUNDED_IMPOSSIBILITY`, `TRADEOFF_FRONTIER_ONLY`, `CONJECTURE`, `CANNOT_CHECK`;
- explicit OCM obligation emitted only after the science terminal.

## Exit

Produce a versioned theory note / field-map rows answering T1–T6 and an OCM obligation list. The desirable outcome is not necessarily a new theorem: demonstrating that the strongest parent product already owns several proposed signatures is scientifically valuable and should narrow the OCM paper accordingly.
